<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">New Haven Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1638 CE – 1664 CE · capital New Haven</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A strict Puritan colony on Long Island Sound, governed by Bible-based law, which was merged into Connecticut after 1664.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1638 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-davenport">John Davenport</a> and the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-eaton">Theophilus Eaton</a> led a wealthy company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> to Quinnipiac on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>, buying land from the local people. They laid out New Haven on a grid of nine squares, and settlements at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milford-connecticut">Milford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guilford-connecticut">Guilford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-connecticut">Stamford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/branford-connecticut">Branford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southold-new-york">Southold</a> on Long Island later joined them.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Only church members could vote or hold office, and the colony based its laws closely on scripture, doing without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-eaton">Eaton</a> served as governor until his death in 1658. The founders hoped for a great trading port, but their richly laden ship sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1646 vanished at sea. In 1661 the colony sheltered the regicides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-whalley">Edward Whalley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-goffe">William Goffe</a> from royal agents.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Without a royal charter, New Haven was included in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut's</a> charter of 1662 and, after resistance, accepted union with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/connecticut-colony">Connecticut Colony</a> in 1664 to 1665. New Haven remained a co-capital of Connecticut until 1875.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/new-haven-colony.jpg" alt="New Haven Colony" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Kmusser assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctcolony.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1638 CE – 1664 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Haven</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theophilus Eaton, Francis Newman, William Leete</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Puritanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Haven</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1651&amp;focus=new-haven-colony" aria-label="New Haven Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2900 705 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where New Haven Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3220 961L3231 946"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3231 946h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3220 961h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3220 961h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks New Haven. <a href="/atlas?year=1651&amp;focus=new-haven-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
